  <subsection xml:id="pp-7-induction-tips">
  	<title>Strong induction hints and tips</title>
	
	<p>
    This proof uses what is often called <q>strong induction</q>, or
    <q>the second principle of mathematical induction</q>.
    Read about it in Section 4.2.
</p>

<p>
    The only difference between <q>strong induction</q> and regular induction is
    that you may assume more in the inductive step: You may assume that any or
    all of <m>P(k)</m>, <m>P(k-1)</m>, <ellipsis/>, <m>P(1)</m> are true (for
    some <m>k \in \mathbb{N}</m> ).
    You may use <em>any</em> or <em>all</em> of these in your proof.
    Typically, <em>not</em> all of them will actually be used in the proof, but
    they're available if you need them.
</p>

<p>
    Below is an example of how strong induction works, in addition to the
    examples in your book.
    Note that this is not a formal proof -- it is just an outline to help
    you understand this new idea. Tap <q>Proof</q> to open and read it.
</p>

<theorem>
	<statement>The Fibonacci number <m>f_{n}</m> satisfies
    <m>f_{n}\leq 3^{n}</m> for all <m>n \in \mathbb{N}</m>.
	</statement>

	<proof>
		<p>
    Let <m>P(n)</m> be <q> <m>f_{n}\leq 3^{n}</m> </q>
    where <m>n \in \mathbb{N}</m>.
</p>
		<p>
    Base cases: <m>P(1)</m> claims that <q><m>f_1 \leq 3^1</m></q>. We know that <m>f_{1}= 1</m> and <m>3^{1}= 3</m>, and by inspection
    <m>1 \leq 3</m>.
    So <m>P(1)</m> is true.
</p>

<p>
    Likewise, <m>P(2)</m> claims that <q><m>f_2 \leq 3^2</m></q>. We know that <m>f_{2}= 1</m> and <m>3^{2}= 9</m>, and by inspection
    <m>1 \leq 9</m>.
    So <m>P(2)</m> is true.
</p>

<p>
    Assumptions: We assume that, for some natural number <m>k</m>, both
    <m>P(k)</m> and <m>P(k-1)</m> are true.
    That is, we assume that <m>f_{k}\leq 3^{k}</m> and
    <m>f_{k-1}\leq 3^{k-1}</m>.
</p>

<p>
    Goal: We want to prove that <m>f_{k+1}\leq 3^{k+1}</m>.
</p>

<p>
    Inductive step:
</p>
<md>
    <mrow>f_{k+1}&amp;= f_{k}+ f_{k-1}&amp;&amp;\text{by definition of Fibonacci numbers}</mrow>
    <mrow>&amp;\leq 3^{k}+ f_{k-1}&amp;&amp;\text{by assumption that $P(k)$ is true}</mrow>
    <mrow>&amp;\leq 3^{k}+ 3^{k-1}&amp;&amp;\text{by assumption that $P(k-1)$ is true}</mrow>
    <mrow>&amp;= 3^{k-1}(3 + 1)&amp;&amp;\text{by algebra}</mrow>
    <mrow>&amp;= 3^{k-1}\cdot 4&amp;&amp;\text{by algebra}</mrow>
    <mrow>&amp;\leq 3^{k-1}\cdot 9&amp;&amp;\text{because 9 is larger than 4 and $9$ is a power of 3.}</mrow>
    <mrow>&amp;= 3^{k-1}\cdot 3^{2}= 3^{k+1}.&amp;&amp;\text{by algebra}</mrow>
</md>
	<p>
    Reading the above inequalities from top to bottom shows that
    <m>f_{k+1}\leq 3^{k+1}</m>.
</p>
	</proof>
</theorem>

<p>
    <em>Notes:</em>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Why did we need strong induction?
                Because each Fibonacci number is defined in terms of the
                <em>two</em> previous Fibonacci numbers, we needed to know
                something about <em>both</em> <m>f_{k}</m> and <m>f_{k-1}</m>.
                Assuming that only <m>f_{k}\leq 3^{k}</m> would not have given
                us enough to work with.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Why did we have to do <em>two</em> base cases?
                Because this strong induction uses the <em>two</em> previous
                Fibonacci numbers.
                <!--, the first inductive step (proving $P(3)$) needs to know that \emph{both} $P(1)$ and $P(2)$ are true.-->
                So this first inductive step amounts to proving
                <q>If <m>P(1)</m> and <m>P(2)</m> are true, then <m>P(3)</m> is true.</q>
                Think carefully about this!
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Why did we only assume that <m>P(k)</m> and <m>P(k-1)</m> are
                true, when strong induction would allow us to assume that
                <em>all</em> of <m>P(k)</m>, <m>P(k-1)</m>, <m>P(k-2)</m>
                <ellipsis/>, <m>P(1)</m> are true?
                This is just a matter of style: We only needed <m>P(k)</m> and
                <m>P(k-1)</m> in the proof, so we didn't say anything about
                <m>P(k-2)</m>, <m>P(k-3)</m>, etc.
                It is considered good style to mention only the predicates you
                need.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</p>

  </subsection>
  
